WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:00.920
Welcome to the debate.

00:01.540 --> 00:02.820
You know, I was thinking recently,

00:03.200 --> 00:06.680
when you inherit like a physical shoebox of your grandparents' letters,

00:07.120 --> 00:09.000
you might just put it on the top shelf of a closet.

00:09.220 --> 00:09.480
Sure.

00:09.580 --> 00:12.040
You don't sit there and read every single letter right away.

00:12.120 --> 00:13.220
You just know it's up there.

00:13.500 --> 00:16.220
And the box doesn't actually ask anything of you.

00:16.800 --> 00:19.080
Right. It just sits quietly in the dark, gathering dust.

00:19.160 --> 00:21.160
Exactly. It just sits there.

00:21.280 --> 00:24.940
And that relieves you of the burden of carrying that history in your active mind.

00:25.300 --> 00:29.000
But imagine if once a month a landlord knocked on your door,

00:29.000 --> 00:30.860
handed you a kitchen timer and said,

00:31.280 --> 00:33.240
all right, you have exactly 10 minutes to decide

00:33.240 --> 00:35.340
which of these letters are historically significant.

00:35.580 --> 00:38.200
The rest must be incinerated to save shelf space.

00:38.440 --> 00:38.540
Wow.

00:38.760 --> 00:42.060
Oh, and by the way, you must pay me for the privilege of keeping the survivors.

00:42.500 --> 00:44.380
I mean, it's a highly unsettling image.

00:44.440 --> 00:45.120
I'll give you that.

00:45.180 --> 00:47.600
But I would counter that it is exactly what happens

00:47.600 --> 00:49.400
when we try to squeeze, you know,

00:49.500 --> 00:52.580
an infinite human life into finite physical servers.

00:52.960 --> 00:54.220
The physical world has edges.

00:54.820 --> 00:56.420
And that tension right there

00:56.420 --> 00:58.740
between the infinite promise of digital memory

00:58.740 --> 01:00.700
and the finite reality of the meter

01:00.700 --> 01:03.060
is exactly what we are dissecting today.

01:03.320 --> 01:05.160
We're exploring the profound shift

01:05.160 --> 01:08.380
in how human beings maintain their personal archives in the digital age.

01:08.980 --> 01:10.460
Yeah, it's a massive shift.

01:10.620 --> 01:11.340
It really is.

01:11.580 --> 01:15.680
Our discussion today is grounded entirely in the November 2026 paper,

01:16.100 --> 01:18.080
Metered Memory, Unpaid Labor,

01:18.280 --> 01:20.640
and the Administration of Personal Archives by Floxchen.

01:21.200 --> 01:22.920
So the core question we're looking at is this.

01:22.920 --> 01:25.340
Is the current model of digital storage

01:25.340 --> 01:27.640
an exploitative extraction of unpaid labor

01:27.640 --> 01:31.980
that forces us into, frankly, impossible predictions about our own future?

01:32.740 --> 01:36.200
Or, conversely, is it a necessary resource-constrained system,

01:36.480 --> 01:38.960
one where these quotas serve as vital reality checks

01:38.960 --> 01:41.720
on the staggering material costs of all this data?

01:42.100 --> 01:42.580
Exactly.

01:42.880 --> 01:43.060
Yeah.

01:43.280 --> 01:45.240
And I'll be taking the stance that Metered Memory

01:45.240 --> 01:48.160
is absolutely a structural imposition of unpaid labor,

01:48.580 --> 01:50.200
and that we desperately need to replace it

01:50.200 --> 01:52.240
with a publicly-funded infrastructure model.

01:52.480 --> 01:54.780
And I will be pushing back on that heavily,

01:54.780 --> 01:57.320
because treating digital space as infinite

01:57.320 --> 02:00.500
completely ignores the brutal material constraints of computing.

02:01.100 --> 02:03.220
Proposing public provision sounds nice,

02:03.340 --> 02:05.040
but it's wildly unrealistic.

02:05.680 --> 02:07.520
I'll be arguing that active pruning,

02:07.760 --> 02:09.040
what you might call friction,

02:09.400 --> 02:11.200
is actually a necessary function

02:11.200 --> 02:12.800
for both our technological systems

02:12.800 --> 02:14.400
and our own cognitive health.

02:14.820 --> 02:16.740
Let's start by looking at the original promise

02:16.740 --> 02:17.840
of external memory, though,

02:17.840 --> 02:20.260
because the current landscape feels like a, well,

02:20.340 --> 02:23.420
a deep betrayal of what digital platforms initially sold us.

02:23.660 --> 02:25.120
A betrayal is a strong word.

02:25.540 --> 02:26.340
But think about it.

02:26.540 --> 02:29.240
If you look back at Vannevar Bush's mid-century vision

02:29.240 --> 02:30.320
of the personal archive,

02:30.500 --> 02:32.360
the whole point was to relieve the individual

02:32.360 --> 02:34.800
of the burden of holding everything in their mind, right,

02:35.160 --> 02:37.640
while still retaining the power to recover it later.

02:38.220 --> 02:40.620
Digital services totally inherited this ethos.

02:40.900 --> 02:41.880
Think back to 2004.

02:42.240 --> 02:43.660
Ah, the early webmail days.

02:43.940 --> 02:44.580
Yeah, exactly.

02:44.580 --> 02:46.420
The early webmail, specifically Gmail,

02:46.580 --> 02:49.040
came onto the scene offering one gigabyte of storage.

02:49.500 --> 02:51.480
At the time, competitors like Hotmail and Yahoo

02:51.480 --> 02:53.700
were offering a mere two to four megabytes.

02:54.040 --> 02:55.580
Yeah, it was revolutionary at the time.

02:55.820 --> 02:57.560
Right, because people were spending hours

02:57.560 --> 02:59.940
curating their inboxes just to receive a new message.

03:00.540 --> 03:02.200
Google's explicit argument was,

03:02.580 --> 03:04.820
keep everything, delete nothing, just search.

03:05.340 --> 03:07.280
But today, the meter has been reintroduced.

03:07.360 --> 03:10.120
It forces users to perform the unpaid labor of pruning.

03:10.120 --> 03:13.400
See, I come at it from a completely different way.

03:14.040 --> 03:16.640
Framing basic resource management as a labor dispute

03:16.640 --> 03:20.100
is, frankly, a fundamental mischaracterization of reality.

03:20.440 --> 03:20.800
How so?

03:21.160 --> 03:23.660
Discarding old records is not unpaid labor

03:23.660 --> 03:26.400
performed for the benefit of a massive tech corporation.

03:26.960 --> 03:29.300
It is a necessary confrontation with physics.

03:29.860 --> 03:32.580
Servers, replication, security, cooling,

03:33.020 --> 03:34.560
they consume vast resources.

03:34.840 --> 03:36.120
Well, sure, they consume resources, but...

03:36.120 --> 03:40.180
But, wait, proposing a public infrastructure model

03:40.180 --> 03:42.800
where everyone just gets a guaranteed terabyte of storage

03:42.800 --> 03:44.100
and unmetered compute?

03:44.680 --> 03:46.880
That entirely ignores the deep risks

03:46.880 --> 03:48.560
of billing capacity without competence.

03:48.760 --> 03:50.280
I wouldn't call it lacking competence.

03:50.800 --> 03:53.140
When you try to make complex systems universally free,

03:53.600 --> 03:55.760
public procurement usually just routes taxpayer money

03:55.760 --> 03:57.880
right back to the exact same monopolies.

03:58.220 --> 04:00.100
Or even worse, it results in stagnant,

04:00.220 --> 04:02.080
poorly designed systems that everyday users

04:02.080 --> 04:03.000
can't actually navigate.

04:03.260 --> 04:03.880
Okay, but...

04:03.880 --> 04:06.140
And beyond the economics, limits on accumulation

04:06.140 --> 04:07.920
are necessary to prevent the environmental

04:07.920 --> 04:09.820
and cognitive costs of endless hoarding.

04:09.820 --> 04:11.680
I want to focus on that cognitive

04:11.680 --> 04:13.640
and epistemic burden first, actually,

04:14.080 --> 04:16.380
because I really think it is the absolute crux

04:16.380 --> 04:17.420
of the user experience.

04:18.060 --> 04:19.620
Everyone listening knows this feeling.

04:19.920 --> 04:21.420
You're at a concert or, you know,

04:21.460 --> 04:22.660
your kid's birthday party.

04:22.860 --> 04:23.040
Yeah.

04:23.120 --> 04:24.080
You go to record a video,

04:24.300 --> 04:25.880
and a notification pops up.

04:26.280 --> 04:27.080
Storage full.

04:27.300 --> 04:28.680
The dreaded pop-up.

04:28.900 --> 04:29.260
Exactly.

04:29.260 --> 04:32.500
Suddenly, you are forced to play judge and executioner

04:32.500 --> 04:35.520
with photos of your dog or old text messages

04:35.520 --> 04:37.380
just so you can capture the present moment.

04:37.900 --> 04:40.420
That friction creates a severe epistemic trap.

04:40.960 --> 04:41.940
Epistemic trap?

04:42.260 --> 04:42.820
How so?

04:43.360 --> 04:44.640
It's just forcing you to decide

04:44.640 --> 04:46.020
what actually matters to you.

04:46.580 --> 04:48.940
Because it demands a prediction of future relevance

04:48.940 --> 04:50.840
that is literally impossible to make.

04:51.280 --> 04:53.360
I mean, relevance is often constituted by events

04:53.360 --> 04:54.900
that have not yet occurred.

04:55.220 --> 04:55.940
Give me an example.

04:55.940 --> 04:59.420
Okay, a mundane, boring photograph of a street corner.

04:59.900 --> 05:02.840
It only becomes historically significant 20 years later

05:02.840 --> 05:04.420
when that corner is demolished

05:04.420 --> 05:06.000
and people want to know what it looked like.

05:06.300 --> 05:06.680
Hmm.

05:07.020 --> 05:09.440
There is a massive asymmetry in deletion.

05:09.820 --> 05:12.240
If you retain a file that ultimately proves worthless,

05:12.780 --> 05:15.020
it costs a fraction of a cent in space.

05:15.220 --> 05:18.520
But if you destroy a record that proves essential later on,

05:18.920 --> 05:20.360
it is gone forever.

05:20.880 --> 05:21.480
That's true.

05:21.640 --> 05:22.600
It is irreversible.

05:22.900 --> 05:23.220
Right.

05:23.220 --> 05:26.940
So when platforms flash a quick-tidy warning to free up space,

05:27.140 --> 05:30.460
they completely conceal the incredibly complex appraisal work

05:30.460 --> 05:31.620
that is traditionally done

05:31.620 --> 05:33.680
by highly trained professional archivists.

05:33.920 --> 05:36.440
They just transfer the entire risk of lost evidence

05:36.440 --> 05:37.800
onto the exhausted user.

05:38.300 --> 05:40.580
I understand the panic of the storage full notification.

05:40.740 --> 05:41.620
I really do.

05:41.860 --> 05:44.240
But we have to look at the alternative to that pruning.

05:44.760 --> 05:46.300
If we don't prune, we hoard.

05:46.700 --> 05:49.480
And hoarding comes with a massive cognitive cost.

05:49.720 --> 05:50.960
A cognitive cost?

05:50.960 --> 05:51.440
Yes.

05:51.900 --> 05:53.220
We can look to Friedrich Nietzsche here,

05:53.300 --> 05:56.720
who argue quite persuasively that forgetting is a condition of health.

05:57.220 --> 05:59.200
It is an act of regulation of experience,

05:59.300 --> 06:00.300
akin to digestion.

06:01.160 --> 06:01.680
Digestion?

06:02.040 --> 06:02.240
Yeah.

06:02.700 --> 06:04.800
You cannot properly incorporate an experience

06:04.800 --> 06:08.320
if every constituent detail remains an object of constant attention.

06:08.780 --> 06:10.620
You have to let things go to make sense of the whole.

06:11.140 --> 06:14.460
But, I mean, we aren't talking about human biological memory here.

06:14.680 --> 06:16.580
We're talking about an external hard drive.

06:16.880 --> 06:19.220
The principle still applies to the extent in mind, though.

06:19.220 --> 06:22.240
If we just endlessly accumulate data without any friction,

06:22.560 --> 06:25.160
we run into the problem of Jorge Luis Borges' character,

06:25.300 --> 06:26.060
Irineo Funes.

06:26.480 --> 06:28.080
Ah, Funes the Memorius.

06:28.400 --> 06:28.880
Exactly.

06:29.400 --> 06:30.940
Funes forgets absolutely nothing.

06:31.320 --> 06:33.560
And because he remembers everything with perfect clarity,

06:33.860 --> 06:36.240
he is crushed by exhaustive particularity.

06:36.700 --> 06:38.400
He loses the ability to abstract,

06:38.660 --> 06:39.480
to synthesize,

06:39.740 --> 06:43.300
to think properly because his mind is cluttered with infinite equal details.

06:43.300 --> 06:45.760
I'm not sure that applies to a computer, though.

06:46.220 --> 06:50.440
A brain or an archive that keeps absolutely everything isn't a library.

06:50.760 --> 06:51.620
It's a junk drawer.

06:52.140 --> 06:53.820
Deletion isn't an epistemic risk.

06:54.060 --> 06:57.120
It's a cognitive necessity to figure out who we actually are.

06:57.460 --> 06:58.780
Look, I see why you think that,

06:58.900 --> 07:01.120
but I have to push back hard on the Funes analogy,

07:01.480 --> 07:05.100
because it completely misrepresents how digital interfaces actually work.

07:05.440 --> 07:05.860
How so?

07:06.140 --> 07:08.580
Funes suffered from compulsory recollection.

07:08.580 --> 07:12.320
He literally could not stop the memories from flooding his active consciousness.

07:12.920 --> 07:15.840
But a digital archive is an external storage mechanism.

07:16.320 --> 07:19.020
It's exactly like that shoebox in the closet I mentioned earlier.

07:19.420 --> 07:19.460
Right.

07:19.640 --> 07:22.760
A quiet, accessible digital archive doesn't demand your attention.

07:23.200 --> 07:25.000
It relieves your attentional burden,

07:25.380 --> 07:27.720
because you know it can be retrieved later if needed.

07:28.020 --> 07:33.500
The profound irony here is that it is actually the platform's quota that creates the Funes problem.

07:33.720 --> 07:35.940
Wait, by asking you to delete old files?

07:36.500 --> 07:36.900
Yes.

07:36.900 --> 07:39.140
By constantly flashing warnings,

07:39.420 --> 07:41.260
sending you emails about your storage limit,

07:41.360 --> 07:44.080
and demanding premature judgments about what to delete?

07:44.620 --> 07:48.540
The platform is the entity forcing compulsory recurring attention.

07:48.980 --> 07:50.220
I wouldn't call it compulsory.

07:50.520 --> 07:52.640
It reintroduces the exact cognitive burden

07:52.640 --> 07:55.200
that the external archive was supposed to solve in the first place.

07:55.700 --> 07:59.100
Well, that assumes the platform's warnings are some malicious psychological choice

07:59.100 --> 08:01.220
rather than just a reflection of back-end reality.

08:01.680 --> 08:03.060
We know from cognitive science,

08:03.180 --> 08:05.300
from the work of Sparrow, Lou, and Wegner,

08:05.300 --> 08:06.400
regarding the Google effect,

08:06.680 --> 08:10.700
that people adapt their cognitive strategies to the availability of external lookup.

08:11.120 --> 08:11.380
Sure.

08:11.480 --> 08:12.580
We rely on search.

08:12.920 --> 08:13.240
Right.

08:13.400 --> 08:15.220
We stop remembering the information itself,

08:15.280 --> 08:17.200
and we just remember where the information can be found.

08:17.700 --> 08:20.580
If we decouple that external lookup from any material friction,

08:20.720 --> 08:22.820
if we allow the archive to grow without limits,

08:22.820 --> 08:25.460
we train ourselves to treat digital space as infinite.

08:25.720 --> 08:28.360
Because to a single user, it functionally is.

08:28.800 --> 08:30.040
But it isn't infinite.

08:30.540 --> 08:32.540
That friction you are calling compulsory attention

08:32.540 --> 08:35.380
is simply the real world knocking on the door of the digital fantasy.

08:35.720 --> 08:38.940
Okay, but if this friction isn't actually helping our cognitive health,

08:39.300 --> 08:40.620
if it's just making us anxious

08:40.620 --> 08:43.760
and forcing us to constantly curate our past under duress,

08:44.220 --> 08:47.300
then we have to ask who this friction is actually serving.

08:47.300 --> 08:49.060
And you think it's serving the platforms.

08:49.520 --> 08:51.700
And that leads us directly to the financials, yes.

08:52.160 --> 08:53.600
This isn't about cognitive health.

08:53.920 --> 08:55.960
It's a mechanism for financial extraction.

08:56.580 --> 08:58.260
Think about the trapped platform setup,

08:58.660 --> 09:00.540
what economists call economic joins.

09:01.060 --> 09:04.580
They deliberately convert your accumulated history into a switching cost.

09:04.860 --> 09:07.480
I assume you're pointing toward logical volume billing here,

09:07.680 --> 09:11.640
but comparing consumer costs to back-end orchestration is notoriously tricky.

09:11.960 --> 09:12.760
It's not just tricky.

09:12.840 --> 09:14.160
I'd argue it's exploitative.

09:14.160 --> 09:16.180
Let's look at the hardware pricing gap.

09:16.480 --> 09:18.740
Look at Amazon S3 standard storage.

09:19.080 --> 09:22.240
The going rate is roughly $23 per terabyte per month.

09:22.580 --> 09:23.900
Which is standard for enterprise cloud.

09:24.300 --> 09:27.560
Yet, if you look at the consumer market for internal hard drives,

09:27.980 --> 09:32.240
purchasing a one terabyte hard drive outright costs only $24 to $41.

09:32.960 --> 09:34.300
Do the math on that.

09:34.620 --> 09:36.840
Within a single year, a user is paying the platform

09:36.840 --> 09:39.720
two to four times the cost of the actual physical hardware.

09:40.280 --> 09:41.960
And that rent is perpetual.

09:41.960 --> 09:44.700
And as you mentioned, the billing mechanism itself,

09:44.860 --> 09:47.180
logical volume, is a total sleight of hand.

09:47.700 --> 09:50.800
The billing mechanism reflects the utility provided to the user.

09:51.120 --> 09:55.280
No, it reflects the platform taxing the user for a space that doesn't physically exist.

09:55.940 --> 09:59.180
Let's explain how deduplication actually works in the back-end for the listeners.

09:59.380 --> 09:59.980
All right, go ahead.

10:00.240 --> 10:01.800
Say a viral video goes around,

10:02.020 --> 10:05.360
and one million people save that identical video to their cloud drive.

10:05.740 --> 10:10.120
The platform does not save one million physical copies of that file on their servers.

10:10.120 --> 10:12.800
Their back-end uses a cryptographic hash,

10:12.900 --> 10:15.700
like a unique digital fingerprint for that specific file.

10:15.900 --> 10:17.200
Yes, standard deduplication.

10:17.380 --> 10:17.620
Right.

10:17.940 --> 10:19.780
It saves the video exactly once.

10:20.000 --> 10:22.480
For the other 999,999 users,

10:22.780 --> 10:26.360
it just generates a tiny digital pointer that links back to the original file.

10:26.880 --> 10:29.020
The physical space taken up is practically zero.

10:29.280 --> 10:29.860
Okay, but...

10:29.860 --> 10:34.040
Yet, the platform bills all one million users for the full logical file size.

10:34.500 --> 10:37.500
It acts as a tax on the very habits that make archives valuable,

10:37.840 --> 10:39.900
like keeping multiple drafts or saving attachments,

10:40.400 --> 10:44.400
while the platform reaps massive physical savings behind this veil of opacity.

10:44.640 --> 10:47.620
I'm sorry, but I just don't buy that simplistic arithmetic.

10:48.200 --> 10:51.320
It's a fundamental misunderstanding of enterprise-scale engineering.

10:51.320 --> 10:55.980
You cannot compare a consumer buying a raw, fragile hard drive on Amazon

10:55.980 --> 10:59.200
to a globally distributed, highly available data center.

10:59.860 --> 11:02.140
I'm not saying it's identical, but the markup is insane.

11:02.720 --> 11:06.080
You are ignoring the immense back-end orchestration required

11:06.080 --> 11:08.860
to pull off that exact deduplication you just described.

11:09.520 --> 11:11.920
You have to at least triple the physical disk cost

11:11.920 --> 11:13.860
just to account for geographic replication.

11:14.240 --> 11:14.840
Triple it?

11:15.160 --> 11:15.600
Easily.

11:15.600 --> 11:18.080
If a server rack in Virginia catches fire,

11:18.420 --> 11:20.620
or a data center in Frankfurt loses power,

11:21.180 --> 11:22.240
your archive survives,

11:22.520 --> 11:25.360
because it seamlessly fails over to a redundant copy in Ohio.

11:26.180 --> 11:28.140
That requires immense hardware overhead.

11:28.700 --> 11:31.440
Okay, even if we can see the tripling for replication,

11:31.680 --> 11:35.840
which brings the underlying hardware cost of maybe $75 to $120,

11:36.420 --> 11:40.060
the user is still paying nearly $280 a year, forever.

11:40.640 --> 11:43.700
Yes, but that baseline cost completely ignores power,

11:43.700 --> 11:46.080
industrial cooling, secure facilities,

11:46.480 --> 11:49.060
and the massive staff of highly specialized engineers

11:49.060 --> 11:51.240
required to keep the system running 24-7.

11:51.340 --> 11:52.540
It still feels like rent-seeking.

11:53.120 --> 11:55.820
Furthermore, look at the market realities of 2026.

11:56.500 --> 11:59.160
Data from ERAC show that wholesale hard drive prices

11:59.160 --> 12:02.520
jumped 15% to 92% over just a few months

12:02.520 --> 12:04.160
due to massive demand spikes.

12:04.500 --> 12:05.820
Wow, 92%?

12:06.300 --> 12:06.820
Exactly.

12:07.420 --> 12:09.000
A consumer buying a drive at a time

12:09.000 --> 12:11.040
is at the mercy of a highly volatile market.

12:11.040 --> 12:13.320
When you pay a cloud provider,

12:13.700 --> 12:15.760
you are paying for an enterprise-scale service

12:15.760 --> 12:17.300
that insulates you from that volatility.

12:17.840 --> 12:20.340
You are buying reliability, instant search,

12:20.700 --> 12:23.180
AI indexing, and seamless synchronization

12:23.180 --> 12:24.360
across all your devices.

12:24.780 --> 12:26.540
You are not just renting a slab of silicon.

12:27.200 --> 12:29.540
That's a compelling defense of the overhead, I'll admit.

12:29.900 --> 12:32.560
But if the costs are purely about recovering

12:32.560 --> 12:35.500
the physical realities of power, cooling, and hardware,

12:36.000 --> 12:37.980
how do you explain data egress fees?

12:37.980 --> 12:41.360
Egress fees are standard practice for bandwidth utilization.

12:42.080 --> 12:45.000
They are a highly weaponized standard practice.

12:45.400 --> 12:47.800
Egress is simply the act of reading your own data

12:47.800 --> 12:48.780
back out of the system,

12:49.040 --> 12:50.340
pulling it down to your local machine,

12:50.540 --> 12:51.920
or moving it to another service.

12:52.540 --> 12:56.380
AWS charges $90 just to read a terabyte back out.

12:56.800 --> 12:58.040
Which covers network transit.

12:58.440 --> 12:58.760
$90?

12:59.360 --> 13:01.500
That's about four months of the storage cost

13:01.500 --> 13:02.760
just to touch your own data.

13:03.200 --> 13:05.300
Meanwhile, competitors like Cloudflare R2

13:05.300 --> 13:07.400
advertise unlimited free egress,

13:07.800 --> 13:10.560
charging $0 for the exact same physical action.

13:10.960 --> 13:12.280
We'll use it as a loss leader.

13:12.880 --> 13:14.520
If egress was a law of physics,

13:14.840 --> 13:17.120
governed by the cost of electricity and fiber optics,

13:17.520 --> 13:19.240
Cloudflare couldn't offer it for free.

13:19.740 --> 13:21.880
Egress fees are not physical cost recovery.

13:22.060 --> 13:23.660
They are a deliberate toll booth.

13:23.900 --> 13:26.260
They are an economic joint assigned to trap users

13:26.260 --> 13:27.920
by making leaving too expensive.

13:28.360 --> 13:30.380
Look, I will grant you that egress fees

13:30.380 --> 13:31.740
are a commercial choice.

13:31.740 --> 13:33.420
And, you know, the regulatory landscape

13:33.420 --> 13:34.960
is actively recognizing that.

13:35.420 --> 13:37.300
The European Data Act of 2027

13:37.300 --> 13:38.880
explicitly targets this

13:38.880 --> 13:40.400
by abolishing switching charges,

13:40.660 --> 13:41.680
including egress fees.

13:42.160 --> 13:44.040
But pointing out a commercial markup

13:44.040 --> 13:44.820
on network bandwidth

13:44.820 --> 13:46.680
doesn't negate the underlying truth

13:46.680 --> 13:47.820
that data has mass.

13:48.360 --> 13:50.220
Data requires vast amounts of energy.

13:50.580 --> 13:51.980
It requires rare earth metals.

13:52.380 --> 13:53.940
So if the market is a trap

13:53.940 --> 13:55.720
built on artificial friction

13:55.720 --> 13:56.760
and financial lock-in,

13:57.100 --> 13:57.940
what is the alternative?

13:58.640 --> 14:00.160
This brings us to the ultimate proposal

14:00.160 --> 14:00.820
we need to debate.

14:01.400 --> 14:03.660
The argument is that we must remove

14:03.660 --> 14:05.660
basic memory from the market entirely.

14:06.120 --> 14:06.740
Here we go.

14:07.220 --> 14:09.140
The proposal is a publicly funded

14:09.140 --> 14:10.140
infrastructure model.

14:10.660 --> 14:12.140
We establish a baseline allotment,

14:12.380 --> 14:13.840
say one terabyte per person,

14:14.200 --> 14:15.800
provided free at the point of use,

14:16.060 --> 14:17.140
funded through taxation,

14:17.280 --> 14:18.680
exactly like we fund public roads,

14:18.840 --> 14:20.540
public parks, or public libraries.

14:20.960 --> 14:22.440
And this is where the idealism

14:22.440 --> 14:24.100
clashes violently with reality.

14:24.240 --> 14:25.500
Let me outline the conditions first

14:25.500 --> 14:27.180
because they are structurally precise.

14:27.180 --> 14:29.740
This infrastructure must be portable,

14:30.240 --> 14:31.260
it must be non-revocable,

14:31.580 --> 14:33.740
and critically, it must be legible.

14:34.240 --> 14:35.880
Legible meaning what exactly

14:35.880 --> 14:36.520
in this context?

14:37.000 --> 14:39.100
Legibility means the underlying mechanisms,

14:39.480 --> 14:40.900
like the deduplication hash,

14:40.960 --> 14:41.680
as we talked about,

14:41.800 --> 14:43.080
and the indexing structures,

14:43.460 --> 14:45.200
must be published and open source.

14:45.780 --> 14:46.680
This ensures the public

14:46.680 --> 14:48.260
isn't just handed a black box,

14:48.420 --> 14:50.040
but actually retains the capability

14:50.040 --> 14:51.400
to rebuild and interface

14:51.400 --> 14:52.240
with these systems.

14:52.840 --> 14:53.140
Okay.

14:53.140 --> 14:55.340
If we align this public baseline

14:55.340 --> 14:56.480
with regulations like

14:56.480 --> 14:58.420
the European Data Act of 2027,

14:58.660 --> 14:59.780
we can create a system

14:59.780 --> 15:00.740
where your digital memory

15:00.740 --> 15:02.700
is a deed, not a tenancy.

15:03.220 --> 15:04.440
It sounds utopian.

15:04.580 --> 15:05.540
It really does.

15:05.940 --> 15:07.320
But let's look at the gaping

15:07.320 --> 15:08.660
vulnerabilities in this proposal.

15:09.160 --> 15:10.860
First, the environmental objection,

15:11.040 --> 15:13.160
which is perhaps the most urgent issue

15:13.160 --> 15:13.780
of our decade.

15:13.920 --> 15:14.220
Go on.

15:14.680 --> 15:15.120
Metering.

15:15.580 --> 15:16.980
Charging people by the gigabyte

15:16.980 --> 15:18.660
provides a necessary price signal

15:18.660 --> 15:19.580
that restrains use.

15:19.820 --> 15:21.700
If you offer unmetered free storage

15:21.700 --> 15:22.420
at the point of use,

15:22.520 --> 15:23.820
you invite massive,

15:24.060 --> 15:25.100
unchecked energy waste.

15:25.160 --> 15:26.120
But it's not unchecked.

15:26.180 --> 15:26.900
People will back up

15:26.900 --> 15:28.720
multiple 4K copies of movies

15:28.720 --> 15:29.580
they never watch

15:29.580 --> 15:30.660
simply because there is

15:30.660 --> 15:31.860
no penalty for doing so.

15:32.120 --> 15:33.660
You remove the only mechanism

15:33.660 --> 15:35.040
that currently forces individuals

15:35.040 --> 15:36.480
to consider the carbon footprint

15:36.480 --> 15:37.620
of their digital hoarding.

15:37.980 --> 15:39.060
I see why you think that,

15:39.160 --> 15:40.360
but the environmental objection

15:40.360 --> 15:42.000
is entirely met by the structure

15:42.000 --> 15:43.160
of the proposal itself.

15:43.660 --> 15:44.300
The free baseline

15:44.300 --> 15:45.960
is strictly cap per person.

15:45.960 --> 15:47.520
It is a bounded allotment.

15:48.100 --> 15:49.380
A cap doesn't stop them

15:49.380 --> 15:50.320
from maxing it out

15:50.320 --> 15:51.200
immediately with junk.

15:51.200 --> 15:52.620
But anything a user wants

15:52.620 --> 15:53.700
to store above that baseline

15:53.700 --> 15:55.300
is priced at actual cost,

15:55.440 --> 15:56.160
including the energy

15:56.160 --> 15:57.080
and carbon costs.

15:57.680 --> 15:58.520
A capped baseline

15:58.520 --> 15:59.220
is actually better

15:59.220 --> 15:59.840
for the environment

15:59.840 --> 16:00.600
because it allows

16:00.600 --> 16:01.680
the power grid to plan

16:01.680 --> 16:02.500
for a specific

16:02.500 --> 16:04.340
maximum energy dropper citizen.

16:04.920 --> 16:06.140
In the current metered system,

16:06.340 --> 16:07.000
there is absolutely

16:07.000 --> 16:08.620
no ceiling on energy consumption

16:08.620 --> 16:09.580
other than what wealthy

16:09.580 --> 16:10.400
corporate customers

16:10.400 --> 16:11.100
are willing to pay.

16:11.680 --> 16:13.280
Okay, even if you cap the storage

16:13.280 --> 16:14.380
to solve the energy draw,

16:14.720 --> 16:15.600
you are stepping into

16:15.600 --> 16:16.700
a massive trap

16:16.700 --> 16:17.980
regarding technical capacity.

16:18.300 --> 16:19.120
Technical capacity?

16:19.120 --> 16:21.320
Yes, providing open formats

16:21.320 --> 16:22.740
and unstructured exports

16:22.740 --> 16:25.020
often results in a practical loss

16:25.020 --> 16:25.900
for everyday users.

16:26.480 --> 16:27.600
Imagine a public system

16:27.600 --> 16:28.360
that hands a user

16:28.360 --> 16:30.840
a massive, unindexed directory tree

16:30.840 --> 16:32.180
of raw JSON files

16:32.180 --> 16:33.100
and media assets

16:33.100 --> 16:33.500
and says,

16:33.860 --> 16:34.600
here is your portable,

16:34.720 --> 16:35.280
letable data.

16:35.480 --> 16:36.000
Good luck.

16:36.460 --> 16:37.240
Well, they wouldn't just be

16:37.240 --> 16:38.120
raw files.

16:38.520 --> 16:39.560
Most people do not know

16:39.560 --> 16:40.600
how to write Python scripts.

16:41.040 --> 16:41.460
They don't know

16:41.460 --> 16:42.540
how to parse and export,

16:42.900 --> 16:44.420
build a local database index,

16:44.500 --> 16:45.660
or host a private interface.

16:45.660 --> 16:47.180
By dismantling

16:47.180 --> 16:48.060
the commercial services

16:48.060 --> 16:49.080
that currently abstract

16:49.080 --> 16:50.680
all that extreme difficulty away,

16:51.020 --> 16:51.900
you are just shifting

16:51.900 --> 16:52.740
the technical burden

16:52.740 --> 16:53.700
back onto the people

16:53.700 --> 16:54.980
least equipped to handle it.

16:55.360 --> 16:56.180
You are claiming

16:56.180 --> 16:57.220
to democratize memory,

16:57.340 --> 16:57.900
but you're actually

16:57.900 --> 16:58.860
just creating a system

16:58.860 --> 17:00.140
that only software engineers

17:00.140 --> 17:01.020
can properly use.

17:01.380 --> 17:02.900
That is a total mischaracterization

17:02.900 --> 17:03.520
of what leaving

17:03.520 --> 17:04.780
the Bayside market dependent

17:04.780 --> 17:05.460
actually does

17:05.460 --> 17:06.540
to vulnerable people.

17:06.680 --> 17:08.120
How is it a mischaracterization?

17:08.460 --> 17:08.940
Right now,

17:09.000 --> 17:09.980
if your lifelong record

17:09.980 --> 17:10.920
is tied to your monthly

17:10.920 --> 17:11.720
purchasing power,

17:12.040 --> 17:12.820
it means your memory

17:12.820 --> 17:13.620
is held hostage.

17:14.120 --> 17:14.880
Think about what happens

17:14.880 --> 17:16.020
during a temporary lapse

17:16.020 --> 17:16.720
in finances.

17:17.140 --> 17:17.840
Someone suffers

17:17.840 --> 17:19.000
an unexpected illness,

17:19.120 --> 17:20.300
a severe job loss,

17:20.320 --> 17:21.080
or a bereavement.

17:21.540 --> 17:22.080
Under the current

17:22.080 --> 17:22.780
private system,

17:23.020 --> 17:24.240
if you miss a few payments

17:24.240 --> 17:25.200
because you're in the hospital,

17:25.540 --> 17:26.400
the platform can

17:26.400 --> 17:27.300
and will permanently

17:27.300 --> 17:28.580
destroy your personal history

17:28.580 --> 17:29.780
to free up server space.

17:30.120 --> 17:31.220
How is that a better outcome

17:31.220 --> 17:31.820
for the vulnerable?

17:32.200 --> 17:34.520
It's harsh, yes, but...

17:34.520 --> 17:35.580
The infrastructure proposal

17:35.580 --> 17:36.900
demands legible systems

17:36.900 --> 17:37.960
with published interfaces

17:37.960 --> 17:39.320
specifically so that

17:39.320 --> 17:40.680
alternative, non-profit,

17:40.760 --> 17:41.840
or cooperative operators

17:41.840 --> 17:43.000
can step in to provide

17:43.000 --> 17:44.580
the user-friendly abstraction there,

17:44.580 --> 17:45.520
without holding

17:45.520 --> 17:46.940
the underlying data hostage.

17:47.260 --> 17:47.920
But who is actually

17:47.920 --> 17:48.700
building the servers

17:48.700 --> 17:50.600
for these cooperative operators?

17:51.060 --> 17:51.660
You have to look at

17:51.660 --> 17:53.220
the realities of public procurement.

17:53.960 --> 17:54.800
If a public authority

17:54.800 --> 17:56.320
tries to buy physical capacity

17:56.320 --> 17:57.420
at this scale

17:57.420 --> 17:58.680
to provide the baseline

17:58.680 --> 17:59.820
to millions of citizens,

18:00.140 --> 18:01.500
they will inevitably find

18:01.500 --> 18:03.120
that only the hyperscalers,

18:03.200 --> 18:04.420
the massive tech monopolies,

18:04.480 --> 18:05.800
have the physical infrastructure

18:05.800 --> 18:07.040
and fiber networks

18:07.040 --> 18:08.020
to actually supply it.

18:08.340 --> 18:09.060
Not necessarily.

18:09.620 --> 18:10.820
Yes, necessarily.

18:11.320 --> 18:12.400
You might change the name

18:12.400 --> 18:12.840
on the bill

18:12.840 --> 18:14.000
from the individual user

18:14.000 --> 18:14.660
to the taxpayer,

18:14.880 --> 18:16.060
but you are still routing

18:16.060 --> 18:17.360
billions in public money

18:17.360 --> 18:19.000
to the exact same sellers.

18:19.240 --> 18:20.520
You haven't escaped the market.

18:20.820 --> 18:21.860
You've just massively

18:21.860 --> 18:22.940
subsidized a monopoly.

18:23.680 --> 18:24.840
Subsidizing a fundamental

18:24.840 --> 18:25.500
human need

18:25.500 --> 18:26.560
through collective bargaining

18:26.560 --> 18:27.900
is exactly what

18:27.900 --> 18:28.980
infrastructure is.

18:29.440 --> 18:30.420
When a society buys

18:30.420 --> 18:31.460
capacity in bulk,

18:31.720 --> 18:32.560
it negotiates

18:32.560 --> 18:33.060
from a position

18:33.060 --> 18:34.120
of immense power.

18:34.560 --> 18:35.820
I think you're overestimating

18:35.820 --> 18:36.820
the government's leverage there.

18:37.120 --> 18:38.020
Remember the Iraq's

18:38.020 --> 18:39.080
hardware data we discussed?

18:39.080 --> 18:40.300
The individual consumers

18:40.300 --> 18:41.700
who buy a drive at a time

18:41.700 --> 18:42.660
are the ones absorbing

18:42.660 --> 18:44.280
the 15 to 92 percent

18:44.280 --> 18:44.940
price hikes

18:44.940 --> 18:46.180
because the hyperscalers

18:46.180 --> 18:47.100
have already contracted

18:47.100 --> 18:48.120
all the bulk capacity

18:48.120 --> 18:48.700
in advance.

18:49.240 --> 18:50.560
A common public baseline

18:50.560 --> 18:51.400
puts the taxpayer

18:51.400 --> 18:52.440
on the same side of the table

18:52.440 --> 18:53.420
as the firms that currently

18:53.420 --> 18:55.060
dominate the global supply chain.

18:55.820 --> 18:56.300
And what happens

18:56.300 --> 18:56.800
to innovation

18:56.800 --> 18:57.460
in that scenario?

18:58.020 --> 18:59.200
The seamless photo galleries,

18:59.400 --> 19:00.880
the intelligent email sorting,

19:01.040 --> 19:01.940
the instantaneous

19:01.940 --> 19:03.000
search capabilities,

19:03.560 --> 19:04.220
these were built

19:04.220 --> 19:05.460
on top of the storage layer

19:05.460 --> 19:06.400
by private firms

19:06.400 --> 19:07.960
fiercely competing for users.

19:07.960 --> 19:09.740
Sure, they build great tools.

19:10.300 --> 19:11.160
Right, and if you

19:11.160 --> 19:12.720
nationalize the baseline storage,

19:12.920 --> 19:13.680
you risk freezing

19:13.680 --> 19:14.280
the architecture

19:14.280 --> 19:15.140
of memory in time.

19:15.680 --> 19:16.600
You remove the incentive

19:16.600 --> 19:17.940
to improve the user experience.

19:18.320 --> 19:18.980
But the proposal

19:18.980 --> 19:20.180
explicitly leaves

19:20.180 --> 19:21.480
the layers above the baseline

19:21.480 --> 19:22.300
open to commerce.

19:23.020 --> 19:23.840
Private firms can still

19:23.840 --> 19:25.160
build amazing photo apps

19:25.160 --> 19:26.300
or AI search tools

19:26.300 --> 19:27.660
on top of the public data layer.

19:28.120 --> 19:29.160
It just removes the rent

19:29.160 --> 19:30.500
charged on the bare existence

19:30.500 --> 19:31.620
of the memory itself.

19:32.080 --> 19:32.800
The public baseline

19:32.800 --> 19:33.460
is a floor,

19:33.580 --> 19:34.140
not a ceiling.

19:34.560 --> 19:35.200
It's a floor made

19:35.200 --> 19:36.120
of incredibly complex,

19:36.360 --> 19:37.600
rapidly degrading silicon,

19:37.800 --> 19:37.940
though.

19:38.540 --> 19:39.600
I think we have to acknowledge

19:39.600 --> 19:40.840
that data degrades.

19:41.280 --> 19:42.280
Formats become obsolete.

19:42.880 --> 19:44.300
The digital preservation literature

19:44.300 --> 19:45.180
dating all the way back

19:45.180 --> 19:45.920
to Jeff Rothenberg

19:45.920 --> 19:46.580
in 1995

19:46.580 --> 19:48.340
makes it abundantly clear

19:48.340 --> 19:49.140
that keeping

19:49.140 --> 19:50.340
a digital record alive

19:50.340 --> 19:51.500
means keeping the means

19:51.500 --> 19:52.560
of reading it alive.

19:53.120 --> 19:53.520
Absolutely.

19:54.280 --> 19:55.200
It's not just carving

19:55.200 --> 19:55.800
a stone tablet.

19:56.240 --> 19:57.280
It requires constant

19:57.280 --> 19:58.240
active maintenance.

19:58.700 --> 19:59.700
It requires migrating

19:59.700 --> 20:00.580
file formats

20:00.580 --> 20:01.280
before they corrupt.

20:01.400 --> 20:02.140
It requires labor.

20:02.620 --> 20:03.380
You can't just declare

20:03.380 --> 20:04.100
it infrastructure

20:04.100 --> 20:05.200
and expect the friction

20:05.200 --> 20:06.140
of time and entropy

20:06.140 --> 20:06.780
to vanish.

20:06.780 --> 20:07.980
No one is saying

20:07.980 --> 20:09.180
the friction vanishes.

20:09.660 --> 20:10.580
The question is

20:10.580 --> 20:11.700
who bears the cost

20:11.700 --> 20:12.440
of that maintenance

20:12.440 --> 20:13.380
and whether they are

20:13.380 --> 20:14.420
doing it under the duress

20:14.420 --> 20:15.580
of a landlord's timer.

20:16.580 --> 20:17.040
Well, as we look

20:17.040 --> 20:17.680
at the whole picture,

20:17.820 --> 20:18.680
I remain convinced

20:18.680 --> 20:19.680
that the material weight

20:19.680 --> 20:21.040
of data cannot simply

20:21.040 --> 20:21.840
be defined away

20:21.840 --> 20:22.740
by public policy.

20:23.400 --> 20:24.360
Friction, costs,

20:24.360 --> 20:25.200
and hardware limits

20:25.200 --> 20:25.220
and hardware limits

20:25.220 --> 20:26.380
are inescapable

20:26.380 --> 20:27.280
physical realities

20:27.280 --> 20:27.880
of maintaining

20:27.880 --> 20:29.340
complex digital systems.

20:29.740 --> 20:29.840
Right.

20:30.400 --> 20:31.640
When platforms ask users

20:31.640 --> 20:32.560
to prune their archives,

20:32.640 --> 20:33.520
they are not extracting

20:33.520 --> 20:34.440
unpaid labor.

20:34.780 --> 20:35.600
They are asking users

20:35.600 --> 20:36.280
to participate

20:36.280 --> 20:37.320
in the necessary

20:37.320 --> 20:38.320
metabolic process

20:38.320 --> 20:39.320
of keeping the system

20:39.320 --> 20:40.280
and our own minds

20:40.280 --> 20:41.200
from being crushed

20:41.200 --> 20:42.120
by the sheer weight

20:42.120 --> 20:43.160
of infinite retention.

20:43.160 --> 20:44.660
And I must conclude

20:44.660 --> 20:45.260
that memory

20:45.260 --> 20:46.620
should never be a tendency.

20:47.400 --> 20:47.920
The true purpose

20:47.920 --> 20:48.960
of a personal archive

20:48.960 --> 20:49.900
is to preserve

20:49.900 --> 20:50.600
our freedom

20:50.600 --> 20:51.760
to defer judgment

20:51.760 --> 20:52.780
until the future

20:52.780 --> 20:54.520
reveals what actually mattered.

20:55.160 --> 20:56.180
By forcing us

20:56.180 --> 20:57.240
to evaluate our lives

20:57.240 --> 20:58.260
under the artificial,

20:58.620 --> 20:59.780
extractive constraints

20:59.780 --> 21:00.840
of a monthly meter,

21:01.380 --> 21:01.860
we are forced

21:01.860 --> 21:02.640
to answer questions

21:02.640 --> 21:03.520
prematurely,

21:03.780 --> 21:04.700
at the permanent cost

21:04.700 --> 21:05.520
of our own history.

21:05.520 --> 21:07.420
While we clearly disagree

21:07.420 --> 21:08.260
on the mechanism

21:08.260 --> 21:09.000
of provision,

21:09.560 --> 21:10.840
I think we do find

21:10.840 --> 21:12.080
a very strong point

21:12.080 --> 21:12.640
of convergence

21:12.640 --> 21:13.520
on the threat

21:13.520 --> 21:14.100
of lock-in.

21:14.720 --> 21:15.320
Oh, absolutely.

21:16.000 --> 21:16.780
The arguments today

21:16.780 --> 21:18.120
make it incredibly clear

21:18.120 --> 21:19.940
that opaque data silos

21:19.940 --> 21:21.300
and vendor-specific formats

21:21.300 --> 21:22.620
pose a severe threat

21:22.620 --> 21:23.420
to long-term

21:23.420 --> 21:24.260
digital preservation.

21:24.660 --> 21:25.580
Agreed entirely.

21:26.320 --> 21:27.560
The regulatory pressure

21:27.560 --> 21:28.600
we are seeing right now,

21:28.660 --> 21:29.940
like the EU Data Act,

21:30.320 --> 21:31.160
moving to abolish

21:31.160 --> 21:32.060
switching fees

21:32.060 --> 21:33.480
and demand open interfaces,

21:33.480 --> 21:35.100
is a vital step

21:35.100 --> 21:36.040
toward ensuring

21:36.040 --> 21:37.320
genuine portability.

21:37.840 --> 21:38.500
Whether the underlying

21:38.500 --> 21:39.700
system remains private

21:39.700 --> 21:40.600
or becomes public,

21:41.120 --> 21:41.820
we have to ensure

21:41.820 --> 21:42.740
people can actually

21:42.740 --> 21:43.800
move their lives.

21:44.600 --> 21:44.960
It is.

21:45.500 --> 21:46.180
The intersection

21:46.180 --> 21:47.040
of our global

21:47.040 --> 21:48.020
digital infrastructure

21:48.020 --> 21:49.540
and intimate human memory

21:49.540 --> 21:50.400
requires careful,

21:50.640 --> 21:51.320
ongoing scrutiny.

21:52.100 --> 21:52.680
The mechanics

21:52.680 --> 21:54.240
of how we store our past

21:54.240 --> 21:55.180
will define how we

21:55.180 --> 21:56.140
understand our future.

21:56.780 --> 21:57.560
It really will.

21:58.000 --> 21:58.440
And, you know,

21:58.520 --> 21:59.900
it brings us right back

21:59.900 --> 22:00.660
to where we started.

22:01.140 --> 22:02.160
The next time you're

22:02.160 --> 22:02.700
at a concert

22:02.700 --> 22:04.140
and you see a storage

22:04.140 --> 22:05.460
full warning pop up

22:05.460 --> 22:06.020
on your screen,

